1.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript contains four regions whose alternative base-pairing controls attenuation. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome does not stall, covers region 2, and thereby permits the 3–4 terminator hairpin to form and halt transcription.  

A mutation that abolishes pairing between regions 1 and 2 leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. Formation of the 2–3 antiterminator sequesters region 3, so the 3–4 terminator cannot form and transcription continues even when tryptophan is high.  

The other options either stabilize the terminator (B, C), increase termination (D), or reduce overall transcription (E) and therefore do not satisfy the requirement.}

11.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader RNA contains four segments that can pair to form alternative stem-loops. The 3:4 hairpin followed by the run of U residues is the intrinsic terminator; its formation causes transcription to stop before the structural genes are reached. Under tryptophan-replete conditions the ribosome translates the entire leader peptide, covering segment 2 and thereby freeing segment 3 to pair with segment 4 and create the terminator.

A mutation that abolishes pairing between segments 1 and 2 leaves segment 2 free to base-pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 is transcribed. Formation of the 2:3 antiterminator sequesters segment 3, so the 3:4 terminator cannot form even when the ribosome is covering segment 2. Consequently, RNA polymerase continues into the operon despite high tryptophan. None of the other listed mutations preferentially block 3:4 pairing under repressing conditions.}

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2}

The trp leader transcript can fold into alternative stem-loops (1-2 pause, 2-3 antiterminator, or 3-4 terminator). Under high tryptophan the ribosome does not stall at the Trp codons in segment 1, proceeds to its stop codon at the start of segment 2, and thereby sequesters segment 2. This leaves segments 3 and 4 free to base-pair and form the terminator.  

A mutation that abolishes pairing between 1 and 2 removes the possibility of forming the 1-2 structure. Consequently segment 2 remains available to pair with segment 3 as soon as segment 3 is transcribed, locking the RNA into the 2-3 antiterminator regardless of ribosome position. The 3-4 terminator therefore cannot form and transcription continues.  

The other choices either favor terminator formation (B), alter only the poly-U tract after the hairpin (C), increase rather than decrease termination (D), or reduce overall transcription (E).}

