5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2

Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome translates the full trpL leader, covering region 2 and thereby freeing regions 3 and 4 to pair and form the intrinsic terminator hairpin. A mutation that abolishes 1–2 pairing removes the competing “anti-antiterminator” structure; region 2 therefore remains available to base-pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. The resulting 2–3 antiterminator sequesters region 3, directly preventing formation of the 3–4 terminator regardless of ribosome position and allowing read-through.  

Options B and C stabilize or favor the terminator; D and E alter expression level without changing hairpin choice. Only A satisfies the explicit requirement that the 3–4 stem-loop itself fails to form.}

10.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2.

Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome translates through region 1 into region 2, sequestering region 2 and freeing regions 3 and 4 to form the terminator hairpin. A mutation that blocks 1–2 pairing leaves region 2 free to pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed, locking the transcript into the 2–3 antiterminator regardless of ribosome position. This directly sequesters region 3 and therefore prevents 3–4 terminator assembly, producing read-through.  

B blocks the antiterminator and therefore favors (not prevents) terminator formation. C changes only the downstream U-tract and does not block hairpin formation itself. D and E alter expression level without changing hairpin choice. The mechanistic consensus across rounds therefore remains unambiguously A.}

12.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. A mutation in region 1 preventing its binding to region 2

Under high-Trp conditions the ribosome translates the leader peptide without stalling, covering region 2 and thereby freeing regions 3 and 4 to form the intrinsic terminator hairpin that aborts transcription. A mutation that abolishes 1–2 pairing removes the competing pause structure, so region 2 remains available to base-pair with region 3 as soon as region 3 is transcribed. The resulting 2–3 antiterminator sequesters region 3 and directly blocks 3–4 terminator assembly, permitting read-through regardless of tryptophan level.  

Options B and C favor or stabilize the terminator; D and E do not alter hairpin choice. The mechanistic consensus among colleagues 1–6, 9–12 and 15 is therefore unambiguous and aligns with the canonical alternative-folding model of trp attenuation.}

